  were away" summary in the page, marked fired, and does [3mnot[0m produce a burst of OS notifications for things that
  happened yesterday.

  [1mPermission is requested on a click, never on load.[0m Auto-prompting at page load is how you get permanently denied.
  There's an explicit "Enable reminders" control; if it's denied or unsupported, the in-page layer keeps working and we
  don't ask again.

  [1m5. Error handling[0m

  [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m unavailable or full → in-memory fallback with a visible warning that changes won't persist. Corrupt JSON
  → keep a backup copy under a [38;5;153m.bak[39m key rather than silently discarding your tasks, and start clean with a warning.
  Notification API missing → in-page only, silently.

  [1m6. Testing[0m

  The scheduler is the part with real logic and real edge cases — lead time, dedup, missed-while-closed, timezone/DST
  boundaries on [38;5;153mdueAt[39m — and with an injected clock it's all unit-testable with no browser. [38;5;153mstore.js[39m is testable against
  a fake storage object. The UI and the actual OS notification are the thin, hard-to-test edges, which is deliberate.

